## Where this script had to know what kind of host it was on

  Each line is portability work this project still owes.

  - CPU brand, vendor and feature flags come from three different places -- /proc/cpuinfo on Linux, sysctl on macOS, cpuctl on NetBSD -- because there is no portable way to ask
  - filesystem type uses GNU 'stat -f -c %T' on Linux and parses mount(8) output in two different shapes elsewhere
  - free space and mount points come from 'df -P', which is POSIX, and that is the only part of this that needed no special case
  - this host has 4 processors but only 3 are online: 1 offline. Check 'lscpu' and dmesg for why -- a core that failed to start makes every timing here slower than the hardware implies

